<!--
  Example ERDDAP datasets.xml entries for the OGF (University of Bergen) glider
  data server, Option A: one dataset per mission per processing level.

  CORRECTED VERSION — the first draft of this file used uppercase short
  codes (TEMP, PSAL, CNDC, DOXY, CHLA, TRAJECTORY) pulled from the OG1.0
  format spec document (SL_processing/OG1/OG-format-user-manual/). That spec
  is real, but it's not what actually comes out of the pipeline: pyglider is
  installed in several of your own venvs (0.0.7, e.g.
  SL_processing/working/pyglider), and its source code
  (pyglider/utils.py, pyglider/ncprocess.py, and the example
  deployment*.yml configs under tests/example-data/example-slocum/) shows it
  emits lowercase, CF-standard-name-derived variable names — not the OG1
  spec's uppercase codes. This matches VOTO's real production ERDDAP
  exactly (see projects/voto_erddap_data_cache/cache_info.csv —
  "temperature,chlorophyll,salinity,oxygen_concentration,potential_density"
  etc. in their actual tabledap URLs), which makes sense since VOTO's
  glider processing is one of pyglider's original use cases. Rewritten
  below against that verified, real-world convention instead.

  Two things to actually confirm once real OGDP output exists, rather than
  trust either source blindly:
  - Which pyglider version OGDP pins (0.0.7 here; a newer version might add
    an explicit OG1-compliant export step with different naming — if so,
    this file just needs a mechanical rename, not a redesign).
  - Whether OGDP's own processing adds/renames anything on top of raw
    pyglider output before a file is considered "L1".

  These are templates, not final config — see the notes at the bottom for
  how to generate the other ~150-200 entries you'll actually need instead
  of hand-writing them.

  DEPLOYMENT NOTE: the axiom/docker-erddap image (see docker-compose.yml at
  the repo root) uses "datasets.d" mode — individual dataset fragments,
  one per file, in erddap-config/datasets.d/, each a bare <dataset>...
  </dataset> block with NO <erddapDatasets> wrapper (the image concatenates
  them at container start). This file stays as the annotated reference
  copy with the full reasoning; the two dataset blocks below have been
  split out as the actual deployed fragments at
  erddap-config/datasets.d/l1_durin_20240614T090000.xml and
  .../l2_durin_20240614T090000.xml. Edit fragments there for real changes;
  keep this file in sync for the explanatory comments.
-->
<erddapDatasets>

  <!-- ============================================================
       L1 — processed timeseries (tabledap, CF DiscreteSamplingGeometry
       "trajectory" featureType — irregular along-track sampling, one
       row per measurement). This is the fully worked example; L0 is
       identical in shape (see note after it).
       ============================================================ -->
  <dataset type="EDDTableFromNcCFFiles" datasetID="l1_durin_20240614T090000" active="true">

    <!-- Once a day is plenty for delayed-mode/L1 files that don't change
         after processing finishes. Drop this a lot lower (e.g. 60) for
         whichever mission is currently live, if you're also serving NRT L0. -->
    <reloadEveryNMinutes>1440</reloadEveryNMinutes>

    <!-- Points straight at wherever OGDP writes this mission's L1 output —
         no copying/loading step, ERDDAP just reads the files in place. -->
    <fileDir>/data/ogdp/processed/L1/durin_20240614T090000/</fileDir>
    <fileNameRegex>.*\.nc</fileNameRegex>
    <recursive>false</recursive>
    <pathRegex>.*</pathRegex>
    <metadataFrom>last</metadataFrom>
    <standardizeWhat>0</standardizeWhat>
    <sortedColumnSourceName>time</sortedColumnSourceName>
    <sortFilesBySourceNames>time</sortFilesBySourceNames>
    <fileTableInMemory>false</fileTableInMemory>

    <addAttributes>
      <att name="cdm_data_type">Trajectory</att>
      <att name="cdm_trajectory_variables">trajectory</att>
      <!-- subsetVariables drives the "distinct combinations" convenience
           picker in ERDDAP's web UI — harmless with one trajectory per
           dataset, becomes genuinely useful if you ever build an
           aggregated multi-mission dataset on top of these later. -->
      <att name="subsetVariables">trajectory</att>
      <att name="Conventions">CF-1.10, ACDD-1.3</att>
      <att name="featureType">trajectory</att>
      <att name="infoUrl">https://ogf.uib.no</att>
      <att name="institution">University of Bergen, Ocean Glider Facility</att>
      <att name="sourceUrl">(local files)</att>
      <att name="title">Durin (Slocum G3) mission 2024-06-14 -- L1 processed timeseries</att>
      <att name="summary">Processed (L1) timeseries from Slocum glider "Durin", mission starting 2024-06-14T09:00:00Z. Sensor timeseries interpolated to a common time base; QC flags applied per variable.</att>
      <!-- Optional but worth it: tag the region as free-standing metadata,
           NOT as a grouping decision — see the "region tags" discussion.
           Any bounding box a researcher draws will work with or without
           this; this just also makes the mission show up under ERDDAP's
           Categorize-by-region browse page. -->
      <att name="region">iceland_sea</att>
    </addAttributes>

    <!-- pyglider (ncprocess.py) writes this itself: dss['trajectory'],
         cf_role='trajectory_id', long_name='Trajectory/Deployment Name'.
         Already lowercase in the source file — no rename needed. -->
    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>trajectory</sourceName>
      <destinationName>trajectory</destinationName>
      <dataType>String</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="cf_role">trajectory_id</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Identifier</att>
        <att name="long_name">Trajectory/Deployment Name</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>time</sourceName>
      <destinationName>time</destinationName>
      <dataType>double</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z</att>
        <att name="standard_name">time</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Time</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>latitude</sourceName>
      <destinationName>latitude</destinationName>
      <dataType>double</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">degrees_north</att>
        <att name="standard_name">latitude</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Location</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>longitude</sourceName>
      <destinationName>longitude</destinationName>
      <dataType>double</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">degrees_east</att>
        <att name="standard_name">longitude</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Location</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <!-- pyglider computes depth from pressure via TEOS-10 (utils.get_glider_depth),
         standard_name 'depth', positive down. -->
    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>depth</sourceName>
      <destinationName>depth</destinationName>
      <dataType>double</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">m</att>
        <att name="standard_name">depth</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Location</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <!-- — geophysical variables: names verified against
         pyglider/tests/example-data/example-slocum/deploymentRealtime.yml
         (netcdf_variables block) and cross-checked against VOTO's real
         production ERDDAP URLs in voto_erddap_data_cache/cache_info.csv — -->

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>pressure</sourceName>
      <destinationName>pressure</destinationName>
      <dataType>float</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">dbar</att>
        <att name="standard_name">sea_water_pressure</att>
        <att name="long_name">water pressure</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Pressure</att>
        <att name="ancillary_variables">pressure_qc</att>
        <att name="instrument">instrument_ctd</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>temperature</sourceName>
      <destinationName>temperature</destinationName>
      <dataType>float</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">Celsius</att>
        <att name="standard_name">sea_water_temperature</att>
        <att name="long_name">water temperature</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Temperature</att>
        <att name="ancillary_variables">temperature_qc</att>
        <att name="instrument">instrument_ctd</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>conductivity</sourceName>
      <destinationName>conductivity</destinationName>
      <dataType>float</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">S m-1</att>
        <att name="standard_name">sea_water_electrical_conductivity</att>
        <att name="long_name">water conductivity</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Salinity</att>
        <att name="ancillary_variables">conductivity_qc</att>
        <att name="instrument">instrument_ctd</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <!-- Derived by pyglider.utils.get_derived_eos_raw (gsw/TEOS-10), not a
         raw sensor reading — not present until that processing step runs. -->
    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>salinity</sourceName>
      <destinationName>salinity</destinationName>
      <dataType>float</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">1</att>
        <att name="standard_name">sea_water_practical_salinity</att>
        <att name="long_name">water salinity</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Salinity</att>
        <att name="ancillary_variables">salinity_qc</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>potential_density</sourceName>
      <destinationName>potential_density</destinationName>
      <dataType>float</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">kg m-3</att>
        <att name="standard_name">sea_water_potential_density</att>
        <att name="long_name">water potential density</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Physical Oceanography</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>chlorophyll</sourceName>
      <destinationName>chlorophyll</destinationName>
      <dataType>float</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">mg m-3</att>
        <att name="standard_name">concentration_of_chlorophyll_in_sea_water</att>
        <att name="long_name">chlorophyll</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Ocean Color</att>
        <att name="ancillary_variables">chlorophyll_qc</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>oxygen_concentration</sourceName>
      <destinationName>oxygen_concentration</destinationName>
      <dataType>float</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">umol l-1</att>
        <att name="standard_name">mole_concentration_of_dissolved_molecular_oxygen_in_sea_water</att>
        <att name="long_name">oxygen concentration</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Dissolved O2</att>
        <att name="ancillary_variables">oxygen_concentration_qc</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <!-- — QC companion variables: pyglider's own convention
         (utils.fill_required_qcattrs), NOT the OG1 spec's 0-4 scheme —
         flag_values are QARTOD-style [1,2,3,4,9]. — -->

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>temperature_qc</sourceName>
      <destinationName>temperature_qc</destinationName>
      <dataType>byte</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="long_name">Initial flag for temperature</att>
        <att name="standard_name">quality_flag</att>
        <att name="flag_values" type="byteList">1 2 3 4 9</att>
        <att name="flag_meanings">PASS NOT_EVALUATED SUSPECT FAIL MISSING</att>
        <att name="valid_min" type="byte">1</att>
        <att name="valid_max" type="byte">9</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Quality</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>salinity_qc</sourceName>
      <destinationName>salinity_qc</destinationName>
      <dataType>byte</dataType>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="long_name">Initial flag for salinity</att>
        <att name="standard_name">quality_flag</att>
        <att name="flag_values" type="byteList">1 2 3 4 9</att>
        <att name="flag_meanings">PASS NOT_EVALUATED SUSPECT FAIL MISSING</att>
        <att name="valid_min" type="byte">1</att>
        <att name="valid_max" type="byte">9</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Quality</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <!-- ... repeat for pressure_qc, conductivity_qc, chlorophyll_qc,
         oxygen_concentration_qc — same pattern, only varname/long_name change ... -->

  </dataset>


  <!-- ============================================================
       L0 — raw timeseries. Structurally identical to L1 above (same
       tabledap/trajectory shape) — only these things change:
         - fileDir points at the L0 output tree instead of L1
         - datasetID prefix l0_ instead of l1_
         - reloadEveryNMinutes is much shorter while a mission is live
           (raw files land in near-real-time), e.g. 30-60
         - salinity/potential_density/density and the _qc variables are
           typically absent at this stage — they're produced by later
           pyglider processing steps (get_derived_eos_raw, QC pass), not
           present in raw output. Drop those <dataVariable> blocks for L0.
       Copy the L1 block above, change those things, done.
       ============================================================ -->


  <!-- ============================================================
       L2 — gridded product (griddap, true regular time x depth grid —
       this is pyglider's gridded output, a genuinely different shape
       from L0/L1's irregular along-track sampling).
       ============================================================ -->
  <dataset type="EDDGridFromNcFiles" datasetID="l2_durin_20240614T090000" active="true">

    <reloadEveryNMinutes>1440</reloadEveryNMinutes>
    <fileDir>/data/ogdp/processed/L2/durin_20240614T090000/</fileDir>
    <fileNameRegex>.*\.nc</fileNameRegex>
    <recursive>false</recursive>
    <metadataFrom>last</metadataFrom>

    <addAttributes>
      <att name="Conventions">CF-1.10, ACDD-1.3</att>
      <att name="infoUrl">https://ogf.uib.no</att>
      <att name="institution">University of Bergen, Ocean Glider Facility</att>
      <att name="title">Durin (Slocum G3) mission 2024-06-14 -- L2 gridded product</att>
      <att name="summary">Gridded (time x depth) product from Slocum glider "Durin", mission starting 2024-06-14T09:00:00Z.</att>
      <att name="region">iceland_sea</att>
    </addAttributes>

    <!-- griddap axis order must match the file's actual dimension order —
         confirm this against a real L2 file before trusting it verbatim.
         Note: pyglider's own ncprocess.py grid/profile intermediate uses
         featureType='trajectoryProfile' with cf_role='profile_id' on a
         'profile' variable and cf_role='trajectory_id' on 'mission_number'
         — if OGDP's L2 output is built from that path rather than a
         straight time x depth regrid, this axis/variable list needs
         checking against the real file, not assumed from L1's shape. -->
    <axisVariable>
      <sourceName>time</sourceName>
      <destinationName>time</destinationName>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z</att>
        <att name="standard_name">time</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </axisVariable>

    <axisVariable>
      <sourceName>depth</sourceName>
      <destinationName>depth</destinationName>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">m</att>
        <att name="standard_name">depth</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </axisVariable>

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>temperature</sourceName>
      <destinationName>temperature</destinationName>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">Celsius</att>
        <att name="standard_name">sea_water_temperature</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Temperature</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>salinity</sourceName>
      <destinationName>salinity</destinationName>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">1</att>
        <att name="standard_name">sea_water_practical_salinity</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Salinity</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

    <dataVariable>
      <sourceName>chlorophyll</sourceName>
      <destinationName>chlorophyll</destinationName>
      <addAttributes>
        <att name="units">mg m-3</att>
        <att name="standard_name">concentration_of_chlorophyll_in_sea_water</att>
        <att name="ioos_category">Ocean Color</att>
      </addAttributes>
    </dataVariable>

  </dataset>

</erddapDatasets>

<!--
  NOTES — how to actually get from this to ~150-200 real entries:

  1. Don't hand-write these. ERDDAP ships a GenerateDatasetsXml.sh tool
     (webapps/erddap/WEB-INF/ once installed) that introspects one real
     sample file and generates ~90% of a <dataset> block automatically —
     axis/data variables, dtypes, source attributes. Run it against one
     real OGDP-produced L0, one L1, and one L2 file once you have them,
     and diff its output against this template. It's generally good at
     variables/dtypes and generally bad at cf_role/cdm_data_type/
     subsetVariables for CF-DSG trajectory files — those three are the
     ones worth checking by hand every time.

  2. Once the per-mission template is right, generating one <dataset>
     block per mission x level is a scripting problem, not a hand-editing
     one — e.g. a small Python script templated with Jinja2, reading
     mission id / start date / platform serial straight out of OGDB's
     missions table, writing one datasets.xml fragment per row. Natural
     home for that script would be alongside OGDP's other pipeline
     tooling, run as the last step after a mission's L0/L1/L2 files land.

  3. `datasetID` here follows VOTO's nrt_/delayed_ convention, adapted to
     your three levels (l0_/l1_/l2_) plus a <platform_serial>_<start_date>
     mission id. Whatever exact convention you pick, keep it mechanically
     derived from the mission id already in OGDB rather than invented
     per-file, same reasoning as the detail-table naming rule in OGDB's
     design notes.

  4. Before trusting this file against real data: run it through
     `xmllint —noout datasets.xml` (or ERDDAP's own startup log) once
     installed — this has been hand-edited twice now and hasn't been
     validated against a real ERDDAP instance yet.
-->
